          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#overview"} Overview
              %li
                %a{:href => "#perspective_of_structured_toposes"}
                  Perspective of structured
                  = succeed "-toposes" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn ∞
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_pages"} Related pages
              %li
                %a{:href => "#References"} References
          %h2#overview Overview
          %p
            %strong Algebraic geometry
            is, in origin, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometric study
            of solutions of systems of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial"} polynomial
            equations and generalizations.
          %p
            The set of zeros of a set of polynomial equations in finitely many variables over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            is called an
            %strong
              affine
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+variety"} variety
            and it is equipped with a particular topology called
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zariski+topology"} Zariski topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideal"} ideal
            in the ring of polynomials over the
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+object"} quotient
            of the ring of polynomials by the defining ideal is the ring of coordinate functions of the affine variety; a basic theorem asserts that this ring is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noetherian+ring"} Noetherian
            algebra over the ground field. This ring determines the variety up to a natural notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            of varieties.
            %strong Projective varieties
            are zeroes of systems of homogeneous polynomial equations in projective
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %strong quasiprojective varieties
          %p
            It is often useful to take a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            along a morphism of fields, usually called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            of the category of commutative rings as a category of affine
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Aff
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Aff}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Spec
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} CommRing
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} op
                  %mo →
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} lrSp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Spec}:\mathrm{CommRing}^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{lrSp}
            into the category of locally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+space"} ringed
            topological spaces. Points of the affine spectrum are the prime ideals of the ring. Assuming the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ≠
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \neq 1
            has therefore at least one point. The slice category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Aff
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Spec
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Aff}/(\mathrm{Spec} F)
            over a spectrum of a fixed field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            contains the category of varieties over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            as a full subcategory. However the points of an affine variety and of the corresponding affine spectrum do not coincide: only
            %em maximal
            ideals are points of usual varieties. Affine schemes have a natural topology, also called a Zariski topology; the ringed spaces locally isomorphic to affine schemes are called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong schemes
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
          %p
            Although
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            in the late 1950s envisioned many generalizations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            theory, his coauthor Dieudonné wrote later in EGA that algebraic geometry is the study of algebraic and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+schemes"} formal schemes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topologies
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Aff
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Aff}
            (including
            = succeed "s)," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+space"} algebraic space
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+stack"} algebraic stack
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne-Mumford+stack"} Deligne-Mumford stack
            = succeed "s)," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin+stack"} Artin stack
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ind-object"} ind-schemes
            and so on; in SGA the study of ringed spaces is replaced by more general
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+site"} ringed site
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+topos"} ringed topoi
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gerd_Faltings"} Faltings
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nikolai+Durov"} Nikolai Durov
            = succeed "\u2019" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+of+one+element"} field of one element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_1
            of various authors, dg-schemes, slightly noncommutative
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-scheme"} D-scheme
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stein_manifold"} see the English Wikipedia
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+geometry"} analytic spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+algebraic+geometry"} noncommutative algebraic geometry
          %h2#perspective_of_structured_toposes
            Perspective of structured
            = succeed "-toposes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            Grothendieck took the viewpoint that the schemes, algebraic spaces etc. are sheaves on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Aff
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aff
            in some subcanonical Grothendieck topology (functor of points point of view).
          %p
            Algebraic geometry starts with study of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CRing"} CRing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mrow
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^{op}
            – main categories being of algebraic
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            = succeed "s;" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+space"} algebraic space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne"} Deligne
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ofer+Gabber"} Gabber
            extend the setup internally to a symmetric monoidal category, where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Aff
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aff
            is replaced by the opposite to the category of monoids in that category;
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nikolai+Durov"} Durov
            on the other side takes monoids in the category of endofunctors in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
          %p
            This is to be compared to and contrasted with for instance
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
            – called (smooth)
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
          %p
            The general formalization of the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            and hence, by the general lore of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+and+quantity"} space and quantity
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stack"} ∞-stack
            has originally been crucially inspired by
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+algebraic+geometry"} derived algebraic geometry
            by people like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+Toen"} Bertrand Toen
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"} Jacob Lurie
          %p
            In particular in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Structured+Spaces"} Structured Spaces
            Lurie presents a general formalism of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+scheme"} generalized scheme
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+algebraic+geometry"} derived algebraic geometry
            just as well as ordinary
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+smooth+manifold"} derived smooth manifold
            and harmonizes with other axiomatizations such as in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            object are realized as special cases of
            = succeed "es," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} structured (∞,1)-topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+%28for+structured+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes%29"} geometry (for structured (∞,1)-toposes)
            that they are modeled on.
          %p From this perspective,
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                ordinary algebraic geometry is the study of
                = succeed "es" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} structured (∞,1)-topos
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+%28for+structured+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes%29"} Zariski or etale geometry
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒢
                          %mi Zar
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_{Zar}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒢
                        %mi et
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_{et}
                on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CRing"} CRing
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mrow
                          %mi op
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^{op}
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\geq 0
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
                and derived at level
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Structured+Spaces"} Structured Spaces
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+algebraic+geometry"} derived algebraic geometry
                is the study of
                = succeed "es" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} structured (∞,1)-topos
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+%28for+structured+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes%29"} Zariski or etale pre-geometry
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mi Zar
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}_{Zar}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒯
                        %mi et
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}_{et}
                on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CRing"} CRing
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mrow
                          %mi op
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^{op}
          %p
            Despite of this, an axiomatic formulation of algebraic geometry along the lines of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi CRing
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CRing^{op}
            and emphasize structural aspects such as to facilitate for instance the transportation or interpretation of results in algebraic geometry to other geometries, is currently hardly to be found in the elementary literature.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SGA"} SGA
          %blockquote
            %p
              Maybe we could talk more about
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry+applied+to+algebraic+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry applied to algebraic geometry
              to unify perspective of algebraic with differential geometry.
          %h2#related_pages Related pages
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+algebraic+geometry"} synthetic algebraic geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contributors+to+algebraic+geometry"} contributors to algebraic geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/books+in+algebraic+geometry"}> books in algebraic geometry
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://www.ncatlab.org/nlab/list/algebraic+geometry"} pages in the category “algebraic geometry”
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+geometry"} functorial geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+algebraic+geometry"} derived algebraic geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
          %h2#References References
          %p
            Original references:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/EGA"} EGA
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SGA"} SGA
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li#ShafarevichVol1
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Shafarevich"}> Igor Shafarevich
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Basic Algebraic Geometry 1 – Varieties in Projective Space
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://userpage.fu-berlin.de/aconstant/Alg2/Bib/Shafarevich.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-642-57908-0"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-57908-0
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robin+Hartshorne"}> Robin Hartshorne
                , Algebraic Geometry, Graduate Texts in Mathematics
                = succeed "," do
